\\), we can assign the lengths of the segments as follows:\n- Let \\(AB = x\\).\n- Then \\(BC = 2x\\).\n- And \\(CD = 3x\\).\n\nThe total length of \\(AD\\) is the sum of the lengths of \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\), and \\(CD\\):\n\\[\nAD = AB + BC + CD = x + 2x + 3x = 6x\n\\]\n\nThe diameter of the semi-circle with diameter \\(AD\\) is \\(6x\\), so its radius is:\n\\[\nr_{AD} = \\frac{6x}{2} = 3x\n\\]\n\nThe area of the semi-circle with diameter \\(AD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of semi-circle with diameter } AD = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi (3x)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi (9x^2) = \\frac{9\\pi x^2}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the areas of the shaded and unshaded regions in this semi-circle. The shaded regions are the three semi-circles with diameters \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\), and \\(CD\\).\n\nThe radius of the semi-circle with diameter \\(AB\\) is:\n\\[\nr_{AB} = \\frac{x}{2} \\quad \\text{and its area is} \\quad \\frac{1}{2} \\pi \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi \\left(\\frac{x^2}{4}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi x^2}{8}\n\\]\n\nThe radius of the semi-circle with diameter \\(BC\\) is:\n\\[\nr_{BC} = \\frac{2x}{2} = x \\quad \\text{and its area is} \\quad \\frac{1}{2} \\pi x^2 = \\frac{\\pi x^2}{2}\n\\]\n\nThe radius of the semi-circle with diameter \\(CD\\) is:\n\\[\nr_{CD} = \\frac{3x}{2} \\quad \\text{and its area is} \\quad \\frac{1}{2} \\pi \\left(\\frac{3x}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\pi \\left(\\frac{9x^2}{4}\\right) = \\frac{9\\pi x^2}{8}\n\\]\n\nThe total area of the shaded regions is:\n\\[\n\\text{Shaded area} = \\frac{\\pi x^2}{8} + \\frac{\\pi x^2}{2} + \\frac{9\\pi x^2}{8}\n\\]\n\nTo add these areas, we need a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\pi x^2}{8} + \\frac{4\\pi x^2}{8} + \\frac{9\\pi x^2}{8} = \\frac{14\\pi x^2}{8} = \\frac{7\\pi x^2}{4}\n\\]\n\nThe unshaded area is the area of the semi-circle with diameter \\(AD\\) minus the shaded area:\n\\[\n\\text{Unshaded area} = \\frac{9\\pi x^2}{2} - \\frac{7\\pi x^2}{4}\n\\]\n\nTo subtract these areas, we need a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac{18\\pi x^2}{4} - \\frac{7\\pi x^2}{4} = \\frac{11\\pi x^2}{4}\n\\]\n\nFinally, the ratio of the shaded area to the unshaded area is:\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Shaded area}}{\\text{Unshaded area}} = \\frac{\\frac{7\\pi x^2}{4}}{\\frac{11\\pi x^2}{4}} = \\frac{7\\pi x^2}{11\\pi x^2} = \\frac{7}{11}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ratio of the shaded area to the unshaded area is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{11}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{7}{11}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"geometry:422"}
